We can compute 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle f\left(x\right)\right\rangle =\left\langle x^{2}\right\rangle =0\times0.2+1\times0.5+4\times0.3=1.7,\]

\end_inset 

have dropped the subscript 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(x\right)$
\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

We can also see that the expectation of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset 

 is
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle x\right\rangle =0\times0.2+1\times0.5+2\times0.3=1.1.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

The expectation of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset 

 is known as the first moment of the distribution or its 
\emph on 
mean
\emph default 
.
 
\layout Itemize

The expectation of 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{2}$
\end_inset 

 is known as the second moment --- normally used to obtain the variance.
 
\layout Itemize

Variance: given by the difference between the second moment and the square
 of the mean.
 It is often denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset 

.
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sigma^{2}=\left\langle x^{2}\right\rangle -\left\langle x\right\rangle ^{2}.\]

\end_inset 

For our example above the variance is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
1.7-\left(1.1\right)^{2}=0.49,\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

Its square root is known as the standard deviation
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sigma=\sqrt{\left\langle x^{2}\right\rangle -\left\langle x\right\rangle ^{2}},\]

\end_inset 

in example 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sqrt{1.7-\left(1.1\right)^{2}}=0.7.\]

\end_inset 


