Distributions as Functions
\layout Itemize

Probability distribution with a simple table can be limiting.
\layout Itemize

The Poisson Distribution --- a distribution a a function
\layout Itemize

First published by 
\series bold 
Sim\i \'{e}
on Denis Poisson
\series default 
 (1781-1840) in 1837.
 
\layout Itemize

Defined over the space of all non-negative integers.
 
\layout Itemize

This set is countably infinite: impossible to summarise in a table!
\layout Itemize

The Poisson distribution is therefore defined as
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
P\left(k|\mu\right)=\frac{\mu^{k}}{k!}\exp\left(-\mu\right).\label{eq:poissonDist}\end{equation}

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 is any integer from 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset 

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\infty$
\end_inset 

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

 is a parameter of the distribution.
 
\layout Itemize

To work out the probability of 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 in a Poisson 
