Manipulating PDFs
\layout Itemize

Same rules for PDFs as distributions 
\emph on 
e.g.
\emph default 

\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(y|x\right)=\frac{p\left(x|y\right)p\left(y\right)}{p\left(x\right)}\]

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(x,y\right)=p\left(x|y\right)p\left(y\right)$
\end_inset 

 and for continuous variables 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(x\right)=\int p\left(x,y\right)dy$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

Expectations under a PDF
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle f\left(x\right)\right\rangle _{p\left(x\right)}=\int f\left(x\right)p\left(x\right)dx\]

\end_inset 

where the integral is over the region for which our PDF for 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset 

 is defined.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Cumulative Distribution Functions
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{sub:CDFs}

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

PDF doesn't represent probabilities directly
\layout Itemize

One very common question is: what is the probability that 
\begin_inset Formula $x<y$
\end_inset 

? 
\layout Itemize

The cumulative distribution function (CDF) represents the answer for 
\begin_inset Formula $-\infty<x<\infty$
\end_inset 

 the CDF is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(x>y\right)=\int_{-\infty}^{y}p\left(x\right)dx,\]

\end_inset 

for 
\begin_inset Formula $0\leq x<\infty$
\end_inset 

 then the CDF is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(x>y\right)=\int_{0}^{y}p\left(x\right)dx.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

In Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:heightPDF}

\end_inset 

 example of the CDF for the students' heights example.
\layout Standard


