The PDF can be recovered from the CDF through differentiation.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Sample Based Approximations
\layout Itemize

Recall that expectation of any given function, 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(k\right)$
\end_inset 

, under a distribution, 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(k\right)$
\end_inset 

, can be written as 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle f\left(k\right)\right\rangle _{P\left(k\right)}=\sum_{k}f\left(k\right)P\left(k\right)\]

\end_inset 

Recall our definition of probability from Table\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:aJointDistribution}

\end_inset 

.
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k\right)=\lim_{S\rightarrow\infty}\frac{s_{k}}{S}\]

\end_inset 

where we have now used 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset 

 (instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset 

) 
\layout Itemize


\emph on 
Sample based approximations 
\emph default 
hope that 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset 

 is `large enough' such that it to say
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle f\left(k\right)\right\rangle _{P\left(k\right)}\approx\sum_{k}f\left(k\right)\frac{s_{k}}{S}.\]

\end_inset 

This formula may be rewritten as:
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sum_{k}f\left(k\right)\frac{s_{k}}{S}=\frac{1}{S}\sum_{k}f\left(k\right)s_{k}.\]

\end_inset 

For 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{k}$
\end_inset 

 a vector of samples from 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(k\right)$
\end_inset 

with elements 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{i}$
\end_inset 

 and length 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset 

 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{1}{S}\sum_{k}f\left(k\right)s_{k}=\frac{1}{S}\sum_{i=1}^{S}f\left(k_{i}\right)\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

This provides us with a sample based approximation 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle f\left(k\right)\right\rangle _{P\left(k\right)}\approx\frac{1}{S}\sum_{i=1}^{S}f\left(k_{i}\right).\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

Special cases of this include the 
\emph on 
sample mean, 
\emph default 
often denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{k}$
\end_inset 

, and computed as
\begin_inset Formula \[
\bar{k}=\frac{1}{S}\sum_{i=1}^{S}k_{i},\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

This is an approximation to the true distribution mean
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle k\right\rangle \approx\bar{k}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

The same approximations can used for continuous PDFs, so we have
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
\left\langle f\left(x\right)\right\rangle _{p\left(x\right)} & = & \int f\left(x\right)p\left(x\right)dx\\
 & \approx & \frac{1}{S}\sum_{i=1}^{S}f\left(x_{i}\right),\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset 

 are independently obtained samples from the distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(x\right)$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Itemize

Approximation gets better for increasing 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset 

 and worse if the samples from 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(k\right)$
\end_inset 

 are 
\emph on 
not 
\emph default 
independent.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Note on Sample and Distribution Means
\layout Standard

In most texts the word mean is used interchangeably to describe sample based
 means and the true mean of a distribution.
 However, you should always bear in mind the important difference between
 the two.
 Later in the course we will also come across time based averages and assumption
s will be made that the time based average is equal to the average under
 a `steady state distribution'.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Stochastic Processes
\layout Itemize

Also known as probabilistic process, random process
\layout Itemize

Can be viewed as a `random function'.
 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Itemize

Normal function maps a set of inputs to a point value.
 
\layout Itemize

A probabilistic process maps a set of inputs to a probability distribution.
\end_deeper 
\layout Itemize


\emph on 
Wiener process
\emph default 
 is the process behind Brownian motion.
 
\layout Itemize

Stock market data is often modelled as a stochastic process.
\layout Itemize

Definition: A random process
\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard

May be defined as a 
\emph on 
random function
\emph default 
 which, for a particular value, is a random quantity.
 
\end_deeper 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Poisson Process
\layout Itemize

A simple process which is related to the Poisson distribution: the Poisson
 process.
\layout Itemize

Probabilities of a number of arrivals, 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

, as a function of time, 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, given a `rate', 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize


\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
P\left(k|t,\lambda\right)=\frac{(\lambda t)^{k}}{k!}e^{-\lambda t}.\label{eq:process}\end{equation}

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

The process is simply the distribution with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset 

 substituted with 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda t$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize


\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

 is now known as a rate, because product 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda t$
\end_inset 

 gives the expected number of arrivals at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Itemize

The mean of the Poisson process (the first moment of 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

) is given by
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle k\right\rangle =\lambda t.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

Poisson process is a form of counting process where the counts are expected
 to increase linearly with time.
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Properties
\layout Itemize

Superposition of Poissons
\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard

If we combine 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset 

 independent processes with rates 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\dots,\lambda_{N}$
\end_inset 

 together the resulting process is also a Poisson process with a combined
 rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, equal to the sum of each of the input rates: 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=\sum_{n=1}^{N}\lambda_{n}$
\end_inset 

.
 This is shown pictorially in Figure\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{cap:poissonSuperposition}

\end_inset 

.
\end_deeper 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ./diagrams/sumPoisson.eps

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

If independent Poisson processes form a single traffic flow overall process
 is a Poisson with a rate that s the sum of the rates of the separate processes.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:poissonSuperposition}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 


\layout Standard

\SpecialChar ~

\layout ShortFoilhead

Example of Poisson Superposition
\layout Itemize

Example 1, Chapter 4
\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard

There are three queues for registration of MSc students from different courses
 at the University of Sheffield.
 Students for the first course, Physics and Fine Art, arrive with a rate
 of 4 students per minute.
 Students for the second course, The Psychology of Sociology, arrive with
 a rate of 2 students per minute and students for the third course, Computer
 Science and Aromatherapy arrive with a rate of 5 students a minute.
\layout Standard

Assuming the student arrivals are independent and occur as a Poisson process
 what is the probability that after 15 minutes there have been 
\series bold 
a)
\series default 
 90 student arrivals? 
\series bold 
b) 
\series default 
180 arrivals?
