collapsed true

\layout Enumerate

Answer
\layout Standard

If the splitting is independent of the arrival, then the arrivals at each
 desks will also occur as a Poisson process.
 For 
\series bold 
a) 
\series default 
arrivals at
\series bold 
 
\series default 
desk 1 will occur as a Poisson process with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{1}=0.20\times10=2$
\end_inset 

.
 The probability that 
\begin_inset Formula $k=5$
\end_inset 

 students will have arrived at desk 1 after 
\begin_inset Formula $t=5$
\end_inset 

 minutes is given by substituting these values into (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:process}

\end_inset 

): 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k=5|\lambda_{1}=2,t=5\right)=\frac{\left(2\times5\right)^{5}}{5!}\exp\left(-\left(2\times5\right)\right)=0.038.\]

\end_inset 

For 
\series bold 
b) 
\series default 
arrivals at
\series bold 
 
\series default 
desk 4 will occur as a Poisson process with rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{4}=0.28\times10=2.8$
\end_inset 

.
 The probability that 
\begin_inset Formula $k=20$
\end_inset 

 students will have arrived at desk 1 after 
\begin_inset Formula $t=10$
\end_inset 

 minutes is again given by substituting these values into (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:process}

\end_inset 

): 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(k=20|\lambda_{4}=2.8,t=10\right)=\frac{\left(2.8\times10\right)^{20}}{20!}\exp\left(-\left(2.8\times10\right)\right)=0.025.\]

