\layout Itemize

Interarrival time: time between arrivals.
\layout Itemize

Think of buses.
\layout Itemize


\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset 

, are continuous values, what is their PDF?.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Interarrival Distribution
\layout Itemize

Example 3, Chapter 4.
 Derivation of Interarrival Time Distribution
\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ./diagrams/interArrivals.eps

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

For a Poisson process, this figure shows the times of the arrivals, 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{n}$
\end_inset 

, and the interarrival times, 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{n}$
\end_inset 

.
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cap:poissonInterArrival}

\end_inset 


\end_inset 


\layout Standard

In the figure above we show different arrival times 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{1},\dots,T_{n}$
\end_inset 

 for the process.
 You can think of these as the arrival times of the buses.
 We are interested in computing the distribution of the variables 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}=T_{i}-T_{i-1}$
\end_inset 

: the times between bus arrivals.
 In this example you will derive this PDF for the interarrival times
\begin_inset Foot
collapsed true

\layout Standard

The interarrival times, 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset 

, are continuous values, so they will be governed by a probability density
 function rather than a discrete probability distribution.
\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Note
collapsed true

\layout Standard

Firstly we define the number of arrivals at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 to be given by the function 
\begin_inset Formula $N\left(t\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 Next we note that the cumulative density function (CDF) of the interarrival
 time distribution will be given by 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(X_{i}\leq t\right)$
\end_inset 

 (see Section\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sub:CDFs}

\end_inset 

 for a reminder on CDFs).
 We can re-write this probability as
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(X_{i}\leq t\right)=1-P\left(X_{i}>t\right),\]

\end_inset 

so, rather than focussing on 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(X_{i}\leq t\right)$
\end_inset 

, we can consider the distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(X_{i}>t\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 Now we rewrite
\begin_inset Foot
collapsed false

\layout Standard

The `
\begin_inset Formula $\equiv$
\end_inset 

' sign should be read as `is equivalent to'.
\end_inset 

 the condition in this distribution as
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray}
\left(X_{i}>t\right) & \equiv & \left(T_{i}-T_{i-1}>t\right)\nonumber \\
 & \equiv & \left(T_{i}>T_{i-1}+t\right),\label{eq:conditionInterarrival}\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset 

where we have simply used the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset 

 and rearranged the inequality.
 Let's take the time that 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{i-1}$
\end_inset 

 occurs to be 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{i-1}$
\end_inset 

.
 This lets us write the condition (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:conditionInterarrival}

\end_inset 

) as 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(T_{i}>T_{i-1}+t|T_{i-1}=t_{i-1}\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 This will be true if and only if the function 
\begin_inset Formula $N\left(t\right)$
\end_inset 

 does not change during the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(t_{i-1},t_{i-1}+t\right)$
\end_inset 

.
 We can express this mathematically as 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left(X_{i}>t|T_{i-1}=t_{i-1}\right)\equiv\left(N\left(t_{i-1}+t\right)-N\left(t_{i-1}\right)=0\right).\]

\end_inset 

The right hand side of this statement expresses the condition that no arrivals
 occur between time 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{i-1}=T_{i-1}$
\end_inset 

 and time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(X_{i}>t|T_{i-1}=t_{i-1}\right)=P\left(N\left(t_{i-1}+t\right)-N\left(t_{i-1}\right)=0\right)\]

\end_inset 

now, because of the similarity of the arrival distributions for intervals
 of identical lengths (Section\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sub:poissonSimilarities}

\end_inset 

) we can write
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(N\left(t_{i-1}+t\right)-N\left(t_{i-1}\right)\right)=P\left(N\left(t\right)\right)\]

\end_inset 

which means that
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(N\left(t_{i-1}+t\right)-N\left(t_{i-1}\right)=0\right)=P\left(N\left(t\right)=0\right)\]

\end_inset 

and substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $N\left(t\right)=k=0$
\end_inset 

 into (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eq:process}

\end_inset 

) we find that the probability of their being no arrivals at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(N\left(t\right)=0\right)$
\end_inset 

, is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(N\left(t\right)=0\right)=\exp\left(-\lambda t\right)\]

\end_inset 

so
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(X_{i}>t\right)=\exp\left(-\lambda t\right)\]

\end_inset 

and the CDF we are interested in is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*}
P\left(X_{i}\leq t\right) & = & 1-P\left(X_{i}>t\right)\\
 & = & 1-\exp\left(-\lambda t\right).\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset 

Now to obtain the PDF we simply differentiate the CDF (Section\SpecialChar ~

\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{sub:CDFs}

\end_inset 

) giving
\begin_inset Formula \[
p\left(X_{i}\right)=\lambda e^{-\lambda X_{i}}\]

\end_inset 

which is the interarrival time distribution.
 The form of the distribution is recognised as an 
\emph on 
exponential distribution
\emph default 
.
 The parameter of the exponential, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

, is the rate parameter from the Poisson process.
\layout Standard

In this section we have outlined the proof that the interarrival times for
 a Poisson process are distributed exponentially.
 Hereafter, we will use these formulations interchangeably, 
\emph on 
i.e.
 
\emph default 
when we state that the interarrival times for students registering at the
 Goodwin Sports centre is given by an exponential distribution with parameter
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=2$
\end_inset 

 we are equivalently stating that these students are arriving according
 to a Poisson process with rate parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=2$
\end_inset 

.
\end_inset 


\end_deeper 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Properties of the Exponential Distribution
\layout Itemize

The Memoryless property
\begin_inset Formula \[
P\left(X>s+t|X>t\right)=P\left(X>s\right)\]

\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

In English, the probability of the waiting time being greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $s+t$
\end_inset 

 given that the waiting time is already greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 is the same as the probability of the waiting time being greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset 

.
\layout Itemize

Mean and Variance
\begin_inset Formula \[
p(x)=\lambda e^{-\lambda x}\]

\end_inset 

 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard

The mean of an exponential can be found to be, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle x\right\rangle _{p(x)}=\frac{1}{\lambda}\]

\end_inset 

 and the variance is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle x^{2}\right\rangle _{p(x)}-\left\langle x\right\rangle _{p(x)}^{2}=\frac{1}{\lambda^{2}}.\]

\end_inset 

 These are easily confirmed --- they are part of Tutorial 1.
 
\end_deeper 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Markov Chains and Markov Processes
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{cha:markovChainsAndProcesses}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Poisson process in a special case of the more general class of processes
 known as Markov processes.
 We will start by considering Markov processes as they operate in 
\emph on 
discrete time
\emph default 
, otherwise known as 
\emph on 
Markov chains
\emph default 
.
 Both Markov processes and Markov chains exhibit the 
\emph on 
Markov property 
\emph default 
which is an assumption about how future values drawn from the process depend
 on past values.
 We will first discuss the Markov property in the context of Markov chains,
 then we will show how a discrete system can be converted to a continuous
 system.
\layout ShortFoilhead

Markov Chains
\layout Itemize

Consider a discrete representation of time 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0,1,2...$
\end_inset 

.
 We associate a state variable, 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset 

, with this time such that we have values for 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset 

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $N(0),\, N(1),\, N(2),\,...$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

Turns in a board game.
\layout Itemize

Markov Property
\begin_deeper 
\layout Itemize

value at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 is dependent only on its value at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t-1$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Itemize

The future is conditional independent of the past (the conditioning being
 on the present).
\layout Itemize

State of the system in the future is 
\emph on 
not 
\emph default 
dependent on what happened in the past if we know the state at the present
 time.
\end_deeper 
\layout ShortFoilhead

Monopoly
\layout Itemize

A real-life example of a Markov chain
\layout Itemize

Forty squares on a Monopoly board.
 
\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename diagrams/smallMonopoly.jpg

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Image of a monopoly board.
\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

Player's state, 
\begin_inset Formula $N(t)$
\end_inset 

, square number +40
\begin_inset Formula $\times$
\end_inset 

number of `GO's
\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard

Normally move through two six sided dice and adding them together.
 Denote the result of this die roll 
\begin_inset Formula $d(t)$
\end_inset 

.
 
\end_deeper 
\layout Itemize

State at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 is
\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
N(t)=N(t-1)+d(t).\]

\end_inset 

 Monopoly exhibits the Markov property, future states are independent of
 your previous states given your current state.
\end_deeper 
\layout ShortFoilhead

State Diagrams
\layout Standard

A Markov chain can be represented by a state diagram just as finite state
 machines are often represented in this manner.
 One important difference is that Markov chains are not necessarily finite,
 they can have infinity states! A further difference is that the transitions
 for Markov chains are associated with probabilities (or in the case of
 Markov processes rates).
\layout ShortFoilhead

Poisson Process as a Markov Process
\layout Standard

We've introduced the game of Monopoly as a Markov chain.
 In this next session we shall relate Markov chains, where time is treated
 as a discrete variable, to Markov processes, where time is a continuous
 variable.
 We will show how the Poisson process is a special case of a Markov process
 where a constrained set of transitions are allowed.
 To do this we will first introduce a new version of Monopoly that we call
 `Coin Monopoly'.
 
\layout ShortFoilhead

